Good to see you. Hey, good to see you, man.&nbsp; Welcome to DeProgram. I'm Ted Rall, and that's&nbsp;&nbsp; John Kiriakou. It is Thursday, September 4, and&nbsp; we're here as promised at 5 PM Eastern Time,&nbsp;&nbsp; as we are Monday through Friday, every&nbsp; single weekday unless we're not. Normally,&nbsp;&nbsp; we are. Okay, so today we've got a lot to talk&nbsp; about, John. As usual, we're always responsive&nbsp;&nbsp; to your questions and comments. Yes, indeed. We love those. A big cyber attack by China,&nbsp;&nbsp; revealed last year and codenamed Salt&nbsp; Typhoon, targeted over 80 countries,&nbsp;&nbsp; infiltrated all the telephone companies, and&nbsp; stole data from nearly every single American&nbsp;&nbsp; citizen, according to experts. That's&nbsp; why the headline today is "China's Got&nbsp;&nbsp; Your Number." We'll talk a little bit about that. I was fascinated by this. War games models using&nbsp;&nbsp; AI models like GPT-4 tend to escalate conflicts,&nbsp; much like in the movie WarGames from the 1980s.&nbsp;&nbsp; Why is that? We'll discuss that. There's also a&nbsp; story out of the Russo-Ukrainian conflict where&nbsp;&nbsp; both sides are using Telegram, which is very&nbsp; popular in that part of the world, to recruit&nbsp;&nbsp; and exploit naive teenagers and children. They ask them to participate in sabotage&nbsp;&nbsp; operations, usually not for patriotic reasons but&nbsp; for money. They use blackmail and deception to do&nbsp;&nbsp; so, ruining their lives. Over 175 child dupes&nbsp; from Ukraine faced charges for attempted, often&nbsp;&nbsp; poorly executed, sabotage in Russia. Finally, we&nbsp; know, courtesy of the New York Post and others,&nbsp;&nbsp; what exactly the feds got out of John Bolton's&nbsp; house. So, what first? What shall we talk about?&nbsp;&nbsp; There's so much to say. I'll add something&nbsp; about this sabotage thing. There was a piece,&nbsp;&nbsp; I think in the New York Times, about three, four,&nbsp; or five months ago, saying that non-state actors,&nbsp;&nbsp; like terrorist groups, are targeting disaffected&nbsp; teenagers on platforms like Discord or Twitch.&nbsp; For 50 bucks or a hundred bucks, they're getting&nbsp; them to throw a Molotov cocktail or place a&nbsp;&nbsp; small explosive device in front of&nbsp; a store or a bank. This is a thing&nbsp;&nbsp; now. It's not just the Russians and Ukrainians. Bad people are convincing disaffected teenagers&nbsp;&nbsp; to do bad things, and more often than not, they&nbsp; get caught, as you'd expect because they're hardly&nbsp;&nbsp; professionals. Reading from the New York Times&nbsp; today, this is how it works: An anonymous user&nbsp;&nbsp; contacts kids over Telegram, WhatsApp, or a video&nbsp; game chat with an offer of money. Once contact is&nbsp;&nbsp; made, handlers provide instructions. Sometimes&nbsp; the instructions are disguised as a geolocation&nbsp;&nbsp; game. "Yes, we pay for photos here," says one&nbsp; ad, asking for location-stamped pictures of&nbsp;&nbsp; police cars and ambulances. This is a quote&nbsp; from the ad: "It's like Pokémon Go, but for&nbsp;&nbsp; money." Sometimes the methods can be darker&nbsp; than deceit. A 14-year-old Ukrainian girl was&nbsp;&nbsp; harassed by her Russian recruiters. They gained&nbsp; access to her intimate photos and threatened&nbsp;&nbsp; to post them online. She became a saboteur. Kids have committed suicide because of that. Aside&nbsp;&nbsp; from the war, it's rampant. When this started,&nbsp; my kid was of that age. He was a high school kid,&nbsp;&nbsp; and I had to sit down with him and say,&nbsp; "Listen, if anything like that ever happens,&nbsp;&nbsp; you have to know I'll be there for you. Don't&nbsp; be embarrassed. I won't care except that I'll&nbsp;&nbsp; want to kill the people who are doing that." I'm not going to be upset with you. Parents&nbsp;&nbsp; really need to talk to their kids about this.&nbsp; Amen to that. Shame on both Russia and Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp; for doing something like this. Kids are so easily&nbsp; exploited; their brains aren't fully formed. They&nbsp;&nbsp; think a couple of bucks is a lot of money,&nbsp; and they don't process the notion that they're&nbsp;&nbsp; probably going to get caught. If they're caught,&nbsp; it's going to cause no end of legal problems.&nbsp;&nbsp; This is a real shame. In the interest of&nbsp; fairness, I think there's a difference, but John,&nbsp;&nbsp; during World War II, resistance organizations in&nbsp; Greece and France, our native lands, used a lot&nbsp;&nbsp; of teenagers to conduct resistance activities. A&nbsp; bunch of them were caught by the Nazis, tortured,&nbsp;&nbsp; and executed. I guess the question that the&nbsp; Russian and Ukrainian intelligence services&nbsp;&nbsp; might ask is, how is this any different? I think it's different. If your survival&nbsp;&nbsp; is on the line, that's one thing. If you&nbsp; volunteer, I think it's different. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp; think the French or Greek resistance were running&nbsp; around blackmailing kids with nude photos to say,&nbsp;&nbsp; "Hey, join the fight against the Nazis or&nbsp; else." These were patriotic young people&nbsp;&nbsp; who hated the Germans and the invaders and&nbsp; just wanted to kick them out no matter what.&nbsp;&nbsp; That's exactly right. They were just patriots. As&nbsp; long as you go in with your eyes open. You told&nbsp;&nbsp; this story yesterday about your grandfather. My&nbsp; grandmother did something similar and was caught&nbsp;&nbsp; and imprisoned by the Quisling government in&nbsp; Athens at the time. My grandfather bribed a prison&nbsp;&nbsp; guard with American magazines to let her go,&nbsp; and they escaped to the United States. But yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp; kids can't process this kind of thing. They can't&nbsp; really consider what might happen to them if they&nbsp;&nbsp; get caught. I think it's very, very dangerous. Over on YouTube, Uout Definitive says "brio"&nbsp;&nbsp; is a great word. It's verve. Last year, mostly&nbsp; Bulgarians were caught in Paris doing pro-Russian&nbsp;&nbsp; actions, like smearing tags on monuments,&nbsp; and they were recruited on Telegram. Wow,&nbsp;&nbsp; you see there? At least that's just graffiti.&nbsp; It's not planting a bomb in front of a police&nbsp;&nbsp; station or what have you. Still, maybe what we&nbsp; need is an international convention. I do think&nbsp;&nbsp; we need an international convention against mines.&nbsp; Not everybody adheres to it, but it reduces their&nbsp;&nbsp; use enormously. The U.S. is one of the worst&nbsp; offenders because we didn't sign that treaty.&nbsp; That's right. I think we need one against&nbsp; drone warfare. I don't know how you feel&nbsp;&nbsp; about that. My God, amen. What makes that even&nbsp; more important and immediate is the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp; drone warfare is the future. It's the present&nbsp; in Somalia. We drone the [ __ ] out of people in&nbsp;&nbsp; Somalia, and it doesn't even make the papers. I interviewed Bernie Sanders for my biography&nbsp;&nbsp; of him, and I asked him about this. I was really&nbsp; surprised. I said, "How do you feel about it?" He&nbsp;&nbsp; said, "Yeah, killing's killing. Who cares if&nbsp; you use a drone or shoot the person in the&nbsp;&nbsp; head?" I was like, "The difference is that it's&nbsp; frictionless. You don't even risk a single one&nbsp;&nbsp; of your citizens to do this. You just literally&nbsp; push a button and sit in a trailer in Nevada and&nbsp;&nbsp; watch someone blow up." I think it's too easy.&nbsp; Speaking of sitting in a trailer in Nevada,&nbsp;&nbsp; I hope that our viewers and listeners know&nbsp; the name Daniel Hale. I think Daniel Hale is&nbsp;&nbsp; an especially heroic whistleblower who went&nbsp; to prison for four and a half years after&nbsp;&nbsp; blowing the whistle on the drone program. He tells a terrible story that is worthy of&nbsp;&nbsp; repeating here. He was in a trailer on an air&nbsp; base in Nevada, operating like a video game&nbsp;&nbsp; toggle for a drone over Afghanistan, and his&nbsp; boss was at MacDill Air Force Base in Florida.&nbsp;&nbsp; They were both looking at the screen, saw the&nbsp; target, and his boss said, "Fire." Daniel said,&nbsp;&nbsp; "I can't fire. There are two little kids with&nbsp; him." His boss said, "Those aren't kids. Those&nbsp;&nbsp; are goats." He said, "I'm telling you, I'm looking&nbsp; at them, and they're kids." The guy said, "Fire,&nbsp;&nbsp; or be court-martialed." So he fired and killed&nbsp; a nine-year-old girl and a twelve-year-old girl.&nbsp; He said that he went home that night and told&nbsp; his roommate, "I became a child killer today."&nbsp;&nbsp; That's when he decided to blow the whistle.&nbsp; Of course, what happened to him? He was&nbsp;&nbsp; arrested and charged with multiple counts of&nbsp; espionage—what a joke. He went through the&nbsp;&nbsp; whole process. He was extraordinarily brave by&nbsp; refusing to take a plea deal and went to trial.&nbsp; I happened to be at his sentencing with Tom Drake.&nbsp; Daniel, if you ask him about it, will say, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp; I'm weak. I'm soft. I buckled." This guy's a hero,&nbsp; a bona fide American hero who sacrificed literally&nbsp;&nbsp; everything in his life so that the American people&nbsp; could have the truth about the drone program.&nbsp; I said to him afterward, when he was suicidal,&nbsp; "It's not as bad as you think it is. You're going&nbsp;&nbsp; to get 15% off for good behavior. You'll get&nbsp; six to twelve months of home confinement. If&nbsp;&nbsp; you tell them you're an alcoholic or a drug&nbsp; addict, they'll put you in something called&nbsp;&nbsp; RDAP, the Residential Drug and Alcohol&nbsp; Program, and you get twelve months off&nbsp;&nbsp; your sentence just for going to the classes." He got out of prison about a year or a year&nbsp;&nbsp; and a half ago. He was on Twitter for&nbsp; a minute, and I was like, "Oh my God,&nbsp;&nbsp; Daniel Hale, welcome home, American hero." A&nbsp; week later, he had shut down his Twitter account&nbsp;&nbsp; and disappeared. All of my friends are like, "Have&nbsp; you heard from Daniel?" No, nobody's heard from&nbsp;&nbsp; Daniel. I don't know what happened to him. Anyway,&nbsp; a long way of saying, if you kill people by drone,&nbsp;&nbsp; it's the same as walking up to them and shooting&nbsp; them in the head. It doesn't make it easier. In&nbsp;&nbsp; fact, it makes it worse. Rebellious Rainbow&nbsp; Unicorn says shooting others in person risks&nbsp;&nbsp; PTSD. That's why the Nazis made gas chambers.&nbsp; Too many Nazis went nuts from killing people in&nbsp;&nbsp; person. That's true. They did have problems with&nbsp; Germans losing their minds when they were shooting&nbsp;&nbsp; people in those pits in Poland and Western Russia.&nbsp; They tried to get them drunk on schnapps. I think&nbsp;&nbsp; they came up with the gas chambers because it&nbsp; was more efficient. They wanted bigger numbers.&nbsp; The problem with drone warfare is, even if you&nbsp; accept the idea that we should be assassinating&nbsp;&nbsp; people on the other side of the world—which I&nbsp; don't—but let's say you accept that. At least&nbsp;&nbsp; if you send a sniper or someone out there to get&nbsp; an eye on the target, the story you told, John,&nbsp;&nbsp; illustrates the point. There's no way a pilot at&nbsp; 10,000 feet, no matter how high-resolution their&nbsp;&nbsp; lens is, or a drone, no matter if you can read&nbsp; the data on a penny on the ground, can see what's&nbsp;&nbsp; going on. It's just too far away. The angle is&nbsp; not right. Drones [ __ ] up over and over. There was&nbsp;&nbsp; some study during the wars in Waziristan, after&nbsp; 9/11, and it's hard to get good numbers, but the&nbsp;&nbsp; guesstimate was that 49 out of 50 victims of drone&nbsp; attacks in Western Pakistan were innocent victims,&nbsp;&nbsp; either mistargeted or just in the wrong place at&nbsp; the wrong time when the Hellfire missile blew up&nbsp;&nbsp; someone else. Ted, I had coffee an hour ago with&nbsp; a British human rights attorney, Clive Smith.&nbsp; He is representing Aafia Siddiqui, which is a&nbsp; different story, but we had the same conversation&nbsp;&nbsp; you're talking about right now. When he raised&nbsp; the issue of using drones in Afghanistan, I said,&nbsp;&nbsp; "You don't have any idea how many weddings we&nbsp; droned just because there was a tall guy wearing&nbsp;&nbsp; white." That was the criteria, or a black turban.&nbsp; A lot of other people wore black turbans. Then we&nbsp;&nbsp; would have to send a million dollars and ask the&nbsp; government to spread it around to the family. We&nbsp;&nbsp; would do it again and again and again. Sometimes&nbsp; we would blow up a wedding on purpose because&nbsp;&nbsp; there was some senior Taliban member there. Then&nbsp; we would blow up his funeral and kill everybody&nbsp;&nbsp; who went to the funeral. The double-tap strike—99%&nbsp; plus of those people were just innocent civilians,&nbsp;&nbsp; local villagers, relatives. If I had a&nbsp; relative involved in crime or whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp; and he dies, I'm going to go to his&nbsp; funeral. Does that mean I deserve to&nbsp;&nbsp; be blown up too? Of course not. It's outrageous. Hey, John, a question for you from Gerasimos Cuts:&nbsp;&nbsp; Off topic, but can you give&nbsp; Greeks your honest opinion on&nbsp;&nbsp; Kimberly Guilfoyle as U.S. ambassador to Greece? See, now you've put me on the spot. Before these&nbsp;&nbsp; comments pass us, I want to say thank you very&nbsp; much, VM, Sodon, Angela, and Marvel. Honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp; we couldn't do this without you. So true. We'll&nbsp; come back to your questions in a second. Kimberly&nbsp;&nbsp; Guilfoyle. I don't even know what to say. You&nbsp; could just say pass. No, I want to be honest. I've&nbsp;&nbsp; never been a Kimberly Guilfoyle fan. The reason&nbsp; she got an ambassadorship—any ambassadorship—is&nbsp;&nbsp; because Donald Trump Jr. broke up with her, and&nbsp; they didn't want her around to cause trouble in&nbsp;&nbsp; his next relationship. It's the sad truth. They&nbsp; decided, "Oh, we'll give her an ambassadorship,&nbsp;&nbsp; and she'll be out for the next three years."&nbsp; Saddam Hussein used to do this. If anyone was&nbsp;&nbsp; particularly popular, especially a military&nbsp; officer who made real wins on the battlefield&nbsp;&nbsp; against the Iranians, Saddam would be afraid&nbsp; they'd try to overthrow him and make themselves&nbsp;&nbsp; president. So he made them ambassador to&nbsp; the Philippines to get them out of the way.&nbsp; It's what the military does. My father was&nbsp; in the Air Force. He would say, "If you're&nbsp;&nbsp; a pain in the ass, they just promote you to&nbsp; the other side of the world or the country."&nbsp;&nbsp; They gave Kimberly Guilfoyle this job just to&nbsp; get her out. It just happened to be in Greece.&nbsp;&nbsp; As a Greek-American and a Greek citizen,&nbsp; I thought, "This is not going to be good."&nbsp; Greece has had a long string of popular&nbsp; ambassadors, going back to, but not including,&nbsp;&nbsp; Nicholas Burns. Everybody who worked for Nick&nbsp; Burns hated him. But there have been a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp; really good American ambassadors. George Tsunis,&nbsp; a very important Greek-American businessman, was&nbsp;&nbsp; the most recent. It's a pretty sweet assignment.&nbsp; The ambassador's residence is magnificent. I've&nbsp;&nbsp; watched Kimberly Guilfoyle very closely as she&nbsp; has gone through the process, not just her Senate&nbsp;&nbsp; hearings, which I watched, but every interview&nbsp; she has given. I have to give her credit;&nbsp;&nbsp; she has tried so hard to come across positively&nbsp; in the eyes of the Greeks. She has said all the&nbsp;&nbsp; right things. I subscribe to Kathimerini, the&nbsp; daily newspaper, and also to Ethnikos Kirix,&nbsp;&nbsp; the English version, the National Herald, the&nbsp; big English-language Greek-American newspaper.&nbsp;&nbsp; People are [ __ ] all over her, saying this is a&nbsp; terrible appointment, that she only got it because&nbsp;&nbsp; she was close to Trump. But the truth is, she's&nbsp; an attorney, a former assistant U.S. attorney.&nbsp;&nbsp; She does have the ear of the president, whether&nbsp; she's in the inner circle or not anymore—and she's&nbsp;&nbsp; not. But she's not an idiot. She's very much&nbsp; a political animal. Before she started dating&nbsp;&nbsp; Donald Trump Jr., she was married to Gavin Newsom.&nbsp; It's so weird. She's had too much work done,&nbsp;&nbsp; and I think that's a big part of the problem.&nbsp; I agree with you, but she's not stupid. She's&nbsp;&nbsp; very politically savvy. It's only going to&nbsp; be three years. We could have done worse. We&nbsp;&nbsp; could have had Nick Burns come back, God forbid. As an aside, there used to be—maybe still is—a&nbsp;&nbsp; newspaper called Pontiki, which means "little&nbsp; mouse." It was a left-wing satirical newspaper,&nbsp;&nbsp; like Charlie Hebdo in France. They might&nbsp; be sister papers. They had a photoshopped&nbsp;&nbsp; picture once of a nude Ambassador Burns on&nbsp; his hands and knees with a dog collar on.&nbsp;&nbsp; How come I don't get to work for publications&nbsp; like that? If I ever go into exile in France,&nbsp;&nbsp; I'm going straight to Charlie Hebdo. The Minister&nbsp; of Education, Vaso Papandreou, whom I never liked,&nbsp;&nbsp; was depicted with a whip, beating him on his&nbsp; naked butt while holding him by the dog collar.&nbsp;&nbsp; He was so angry he lodged an official protest.&nbsp; It's like, dude, it's a satirical newspaper.&nbsp;&nbsp; Have you never heard of freedom of speech and&nbsp; freedom of the press? So we all went out and&nbsp;&nbsp; bought these newspapers. It was the back cover&nbsp; of the paper that day, and we all taped them up&nbsp;&nbsp; in front of our desks. He didn't have access&nbsp; to the station because he was the ambassador,&nbsp;&nbsp; not a station employee, so he never saw it. I'm not confirming or denying the existence&nbsp;&nbsp; of a station in Athens. That's where I put up my&nbsp; thing. We've got some questions to answer from&nbsp;&nbsp; people who have kindly paid. VM, thank you&nbsp; so much for the 50 euros. Avid fan, I don't&nbsp;&nbsp; miss a day. I’d like your thoughts on Belgian&nbsp; intelligence at some point, if you have any.&nbsp; You know what? I could make jokes about&nbsp; how Belgian intelligence is an oxymoron,&nbsp;&nbsp; but I do have to tell you a Belgian story. It's&nbsp; not about intelligence, but it reminds me of this.&nbsp;&nbsp; I love stories about incredibly stupid people. My&nbsp; ex-wife and I were on a bullet train from Paris to&nbsp;&nbsp; Amsterdam, which goes through Brussels and the&nbsp; Netherlands. These two douchebag bros, about&nbsp;&nbsp; 22 or 23 years old, got on with big duffels and&nbsp; left them in the middle of the aisle where people&nbsp;&nbsp; were trying to walk. Several times, the conductor&nbsp; came by and told them, in Flemish or whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp; to get that [ __ ] out of there. Each time, they&nbsp; pulled them out, and as soon as the guy was gone,&nbsp;&nbsp; they put them back in the aisle. This happened&nbsp; a number of times. The conductor was getting&nbsp;&nbsp; more and more angry. Finally, he’d had enough. The train stopped, and some policemen came on&nbsp;&nbsp; with the conductor. He pointed to the two guys,&nbsp; the cops confronted them, opened the bags, and&nbsp;&nbsp; they were full of weapons—big guns. I’m like, you&nbsp; couldn’t keep them on your lap? They were guns!&nbsp; [ __ ] Belgians. What do you think about Belgian&nbsp; intelligence? This is one of those small-world&nbsp;&nbsp; stories. When I was a senior in high school, we&nbsp; had a foreign exchange student from Brussels,&nbsp;&nbsp; Pete Jambon. Pete and I are still close buddies.&nbsp; He comes back to the States just about every year.&nbsp;&nbsp; We’re in each other’s weddings and godfathers to&nbsp; each other’s kids. Pete and my three other closest&nbsp;&nbsp; friends—Pete went into private business and became&nbsp; very wealthy. His brother went into government and&nbsp;&nbsp; became the director of the Belgian intelligence&nbsp; service. The last time he was here in the States,&nbsp;&nbsp; we met up in Atlanta to go to another one of&nbsp; our best friends’ son’s wedding. I said, “So&nbsp;&nbsp; how’s your brother doing in this intel director&nbsp; slot?” He said, “Oh, they made him intel director&nbsp;&nbsp; and deputy prime minister. It’s the craziest&nbsp; thing.” I said, “Oh my God, how’s he fitting in&nbsp;&nbsp; with intelligence?” He said, “I never realized&nbsp; my brother had a hatred for Arabs.” I’m like,&nbsp;&nbsp; “What? You guys don’t hate anybody.” He says,&nbsp; “Every time I call him on it, he says, ‘Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp; you don’t see what I see. If you could see what&nbsp; I see,’” I said, “Oh, Pete, I hear that all the&nbsp;&nbsp; time. ‘If you had access to what I have access to,&nbsp; you’d want to kill everybody.’ Well, no, I used to&nbsp;&nbsp; have access to what you had access to, and I never&nbsp; wanted to kill anybody.” But the Belgians are very&nbsp;&nbsp; good when it comes to Arabs, and they pretty much&nbsp; ignore everything else. They’re panicked at the&nbsp;&nbsp; idea that Palestinian refugees, for example—there&nbsp; are a lot of Palestinian refugees in Belgium—might&nbsp;&nbsp; be terrorists. France had that spate of PLO&nbsp; bombings and other Islamic Jihad bombings. They&nbsp;&nbsp; got rid of all the trash cans and mailboxes, just&nbsp; like we did. Did Belgium have the same issue? Yes.&nbsp; Reed makes a great point. I totally forgot about&nbsp; that. Fauda Season 4 was filmed in Belgium. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp; exactly right. Reed, I remember this too.&nbsp; She’s asking—I assume she’s a she—wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp; Guilfoyle accused of sexually harassing a&nbsp; male intern when she worked at Fox? I seem&nbsp;&nbsp; to remember that. I think I do too. I don’t&nbsp; remember what the final result was, but yeah.&nbsp; Saucy. Spicy. Sodon, thanks for the 20 bucks.&nbsp; Hey, John and Ted, can you please give us some&nbsp;&nbsp; insight as to why the Brits allowed China&nbsp; to build a huge embassy near the financial&nbsp;&nbsp; district with sub-level fiber optics? Should the&nbsp; UK and the U.S. be concerned? It’s a huge complex.&nbsp; Well, you know the story about the American&nbsp; embassy in Moscow and the Russian embassy in&nbsp;&nbsp; Washington. They were both built around the same&nbsp; time. The deal was struck in the 1970s that both&nbsp;&nbsp; countries would build new embassies. We told&nbsp; the Russians, “Go ahead and find a plot of land&nbsp;&nbsp; and pick what you want.” So they went to literally&nbsp; the highest point in Washington. I wonder why—they&nbsp;&nbsp; just wanted to get Channel 2 and Channel 7. Good&nbsp; reception. Oh, by the way, you can also see the&nbsp;&nbsp; White House and the Pentagon from up there, and&nbsp; signals intelligence is wonderful. The Russians&nbsp;&nbsp; allowed us to purchase a dry riverbed in Moscow&nbsp; that was in this culvert in the middle of Russia.&nbsp; We ended up having to tear the new&nbsp; embassy down because the Russians had&nbsp;&nbsp; put listening devices in literally every beam&nbsp; and every two-by-four in the entire embassy.&nbsp;&nbsp; Just pulling the wires out would have made the&nbsp; building collapse. So we would not allow them&nbsp;&nbsp; to occupy their new embassy, which is way up at&nbsp; the top of Wisconsin Avenue, just immediately&nbsp;&nbsp; south of the National Cathedral. You can see the&nbsp; Russian embassy from everywhere in Washington.&nbsp; I love these shenanigans, though. I had a college&nbsp; girlfriend who was a diplomatic brat. She grew up&nbsp;&nbsp; at the American Embassy in Moscow during the&nbsp; 1970s and 1980s. She said there were always&nbsp;&nbsp; shenanigans. All the cleaning staff were KGB, no&nbsp; question about it. There were constantly fires,&nbsp;&nbsp; so everyone had to leave. You knew the&nbsp; firefighters were all KGB, and they were going in&nbsp;&nbsp; there planting bugs. Everything had to&nbsp; be swept over and over. They knew they&nbsp;&nbsp; weren’t catching them all. They were also really&nbsp; concerned about cancer because the Russians were&nbsp;&nbsp; beaming microwaves at the embassy, and people were&nbsp; getting cancer. My understanding is that if you&nbsp;&nbsp; talk inside a room, you can pick up the vibration&nbsp; off the glass in the window. The microwaves allow&nbsp;&nbsp; you to hear what’s going on inside. Ted, when&nbsp; I was living in Bahrain, which is joined at the&nbsp;&nbsp; hip with the United States defense and foreign&nbsp; policy-wise, I was the human rights officer. One&nbsp;&nbsp; night, I’m lying in bed with my wife, reading a&nbsp; book. It was really hot, so I had no shirt on.&nbsp; This thing falls onto my chest. I’m&nbsp; like, “What the [ __ ] is this?” I look up,&nbsp;&nbsp; and the air conditioning vent is directly&nbsp; above me. I pick this thing up—it’s clearly&nbsp;&nbsp; a bug with wires sticking out and a little&nbsp; watch battery on it. I put it in a Ziploc bag.&nbsp; The next day, I took it to the embassy and showed&nbsp; it to my chief. He holds it up and says, “Yep,&nbsp;&nbsp; it’s a bug.” I said, “Well, now what am I supposed&nbsp; to do?” My wife almost never leaves the house,&nbsp;&nbsp; but she must have gone grocery shopping or taken&nbsp; our son to the park, and they immediately went&nbsp;&nbsp; in and planted this bug in the bedroom, no less. In the bedroom. That’s bad. My wife was a ballet&nbsp;&nbsp; teacher, not a CIA officer. I would never discuss&nbsp; anything with her because she wasn’t cleared.&nbsp;&nbsp; He said, “It’s because you’re writing that damn&nbsp; human rights report. They don’t like you one bit.”&nbsp;&nbsp; The Bahrainis used to do this: whenever your&nbsp; tour was up, we’re not allowed to accept any&nbsp;&nbsp; gift worth more than $25. So they would send&nbsp; everybody a Rolex. You had to report it to the&nbsp;&nbsp; Treasury Department, and you could either&nbsp; turn it over or bid on it and buy it back.&nbsp;&nbsp; I was the only one in the entire embassy who&nbsp; didn’t get a Rolex when I left. I didn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp; get a “thank you, come again.” They hated me. I would go into the Minister of Interior’s office&nbsp;&nbsp; and say, “Your Highness, you cannot just pick&nbsp; up a 15-year-old kid off the street for marching&nbsp;&nbsp; in a pro-democracy demonstration, beat him to&nbsp; death, and then call his parents to come pick&nbsp;&nbsp; up the body. You can’t do that. I have to report&nbsp; that to Congress, and it’s going to jeopardize&nbsp;&nbsp; the sale of weapons to Bahrain. Back off.” Angela, thanks for the donation. Marble,&nbsp;&nbsp; I love this question: Can someone please&nbsp; explain how Laura Loomer is so influential?&nbsp;&nbsp; Seriously. I don’t know. If someone has&nbsp; the answer, I honestly don’t know. Listen,&nbsp;&nbsp; Laura Loomer. We have a mutual friend, and I said&nbsp; to the mutual friend, “Why do you even associate&nbsp;&nbsp; with somebody like Laura Loomer?” First of all,&nbsp; she’s the only person I’ve ever heard of who’s&nbsp;&nbsp; banned by Uber, Lyft, and Target for hate&nbsp; speech. She can’t go to Target. I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp; “How can you be friends with somebody like that?”&nbsp; “Oh, no, she’s misunderstood.” No, she’s very well&nbsp;&nbsp; understood. That’s the problem here. She ran&nbsp; for Congress in either 2020 or 2022—I can’t&nbsp;&nbsp; remember—in Donald Trump’s home district of Palm&nbsp; Beach and got 24%. Even Republicans realize she’s&nbsp;&nbsp; nuts and so far to the right as to be unhinged. She has this inexplicable influence over the&nbsp;&nbsp; president of the United States. I don’t&nbsp; understand it. Maybe she made some&nbsp;&nbsp; appearance on Fox that impressed him one time or&nbsp; something like that. It’s often like that. John,&nbsp;&nbsp; Star-Lions is asking, “I thought you were really&nbsp; connected with Bahraini royalty. If that’s true,&nbsp;&nbsp; why would the security service still treat you&nbsp; like crap?” The Crown Prince and I still text&nbsp;&nbsp; each other jokes. I heard from him on Saturday,&nbsp; as a matter of fact. But when I was in Bahrain,&nbsp;&nbsp; the director of the Bahraini intelligence&nbsp; service was General Ian Henderson.&nbsp; General Ian Henderson was the British colonialist&nbsp; who put down the Mau Mau rebellion in Rhodesia&nbsp;&nbsp; and did everything he could to keep black&nbsp; Rhodesians out of power until he had to flee. He&nbsp;&nbsp; went to Bahrain, and they said, “Hey, can&nbsp; you set up an intelligence service for us?”&nbsp; Ian Henderson was the worst kind of colonial&nbsp; Brit, to the point where even the Bahrainis&nbsp;&nbsp; were afraid of him. I’ll tell you another thing.&nbsp; George H.W. Bush came to Bahrain when I was there,&nbsp;&nbsp; and the emir had a gigantic dinner for&nbsp; him. The crown prince asked that I be&nbsp;&nbsp; seated at his table. We’re sitting there,&nbsp; laughing. I was young at the time—I was 29.&nbsp; We’re laughing and joking, and I said, “Who’s&nbsp; the corpse over at this other table?” He turns&nbsp;&nbsp; and says, “Seriously?” I said, “Yeah, it’s this&nbsp; 80-year-old man wearing a brown leisure suit with&nbsp;&nbsp; a loud flower shirt underneath and big wide collar&nbsp; with wide lapels from the seventies.” I said,&nbsp;&nbsp; “Who’s the stiff?” He says, “That’s General&nbsp; Henderson.” I go, “That’s Ian Henderson?” I said,&nbsp;&nbsp; “He looks like he crawled right out of the&nbsp; seventies.” He’s like, “Yeah, you know what?&nbsp;&nbsp; We don’t talk about General Henderson.” Even&nbsp; the heir presumptive, whose grandfather was&nbsp;&nbsp; the emir and whose father was the crown prince,&nbsp; was afraid of Ian Henderson to the point where he&nbsp;&nbsp; didn’t even want to speak the man’s name. I’m dying to get your thoughts about this&nbsp;&nbsp; war games thing. There’s an article in Politico&nbsp; about the AI doomsday machine. The argument is&nbsp;&nbsp; that autonomous warfare—self-autonomous weapons&nbsp; that choose their own targets using AI—is the&nbsp;&nbsp; future. There’s no way to ignore it. If we don’t&nbsp; do it, the Chinese will. The Russians will. We&nbsp;&nbsp; have to keep up. DARPA, the big research arm of&nbsp; the Pentagon, is keeping up. The problem they’re&nbsp;&nbsp; finding is that ChatGPT and other AI models,&nbsp; when faced with a military dilemma, situation, or&nbsp;&nbsp; crisis, tend to escalate rather than de-escalate. You don’t have to have seen The Terminator a&nbsp;&nbsp; million times like I have to know how this works&nbsp; out. I have a theory as to why this is true,&nbsp;&nbsp; John, but I want to hear yours first. Ted,&nbsp; this is Black Mirror come to life. It really&nbsp;&nbsp; is the future. I hate to say it, and it&nbsp; scares the hell out of me. Those robot&nbsp;&nbsp; dogs from that Black Mirror episode are real. They look exactly like that. If DARPA can perfect&nbsp;&nbsp; a battery with a long enough life to attach to a&nbsp; robot to fight our wars, everything changes. We’re&nbsp;&nbsp; quickly getting to a point where the ultimate&nbsp; goal is not to send human beings into battle&nbsp;&nbsp; on our side. It’s to send robots, robot dogs,&nbsp; drones, all powered by AI, to fight our wars&nbsp;&nbsp; without expending a single life. Let me add one&nbsp; thing. Many of you know I’m a huge fan of really&nbsp;&nbsp; bad 60s and 70s television. I’m addicted to it&nbsp; right now. I’ve got Dragnet, Adam-12, and Star&nbsp;&nbsp; Trek—only the original Star Trek—in my queue. There’s a Star Trek episode apropos of this&nbsp;&nbsp; conversation. Kirk and Spock go to this&nbsp; planet where they hate the people on&nbsp;&nbsp; the next planet. These planets are&nbsp; really close together. One planet,&nbsp;&nbsp; Planet A, calls Planet B and says, “Listen, we’re&nbsp; launching an attack on you guys. Here it comes.”&nbsp; It impacted at such-and-such a coordinate. Planet&nbsp; B says, “Oh crap, you killed 3,500 people.” But&nbsp;&nbsp; they didn’t really—what they were doing was&nbsp; computer exercises. If the computer said it was&nbsp;&nbsp; a direct hit that killed 35 people, they would&nbsp; send 35 people to death chambers to be put to&nbsp;&nbsp; death so they didn’t have to keep rebuilding&nbsp; buildings. They’d launch a counterattack: “We&nbsp;&nbsp; killed 10,000 people in the counterattack.” So now&nbsp; you have to send 10,000 people. It’s a simulation,&nbsp;&nbsp; but you really do kill the people after the&nbsp; fact. In ancient history, I don’t remember&nbsp;&nbsp; if it was the Assyrians or the Hittites or both,&nbsp; they got tired of destroying each other’s cities.&nbsp; They would line up the armies side by side, and&nbsp; a military CPA would count all the men, weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp; and assess their training. They’d negotiate and&nbsp; say, “In this battle, the Hittites would have&nbsp;&nbsp; won.” So nobody’s going to kill anyone; we’re&nbsp; all going home to our wives and kids. But we’ll&nbsp;&nbsp; cede this amount of territory or money. That’s&nbsp; how they used to do it. It sounds civilized,&nbsp;&nbsp; I guess. My theory is that ChatGPT, Grok, Google&nbsp; AI—they’re all trained by illegally scraping&nbsp;&nbsp; everything they can from the internet with spiders&nbsp; and bots. They’re getting basically the entire&nbsp;&nbsp; internet. What’s the internet? It’s the expression&nbsp; of our political and cultural discourse. Our&nbsp;&nbsp; culture is always escalatory, whether in&nbsp; war or in relationships. If someone says,&nbsp;&nbsp; “My husband forgot my anniversary,” the response&nbsp; is, “Divorce him!” Everything goes to 11 in our&nbsp;&nbsp; culture. Think about a military or foreign crisis.&nbsp; When’s the last time you watched CNN or MSNBC and&nbsp;&nbsp; saw a single panel member say, “Maybe the U.S.&nbsp; shouldn’t be involved. It’s not our business,” or,&nbsp;&nbsp; “We should try to negotiate peace”? I’m 62 years&nbsp; old. In tens of thousands of hours of cable news,&nbsp;&nbsp; I’ve never heard a single person say, “This is&nbsp; not our business,” or, “We should de-escalate&nbsp;&nbsp; this situation.” So I think what’s happening&nbsp; is that the bots are trained on an escalatory&nbsp;&nbsp; culture. Of course, they escalate everything. Why haven’t we put together intellectually&nbsp;&nbsp; that they are automatically escalatory,&nbsp; convincing teenagers to kill themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp; and that’s a problem? In combination, it’s going&nbsp; to be a very big problem. I say all the time,&nbsp;&nbsp; only half-jokingly, Ted, I saw I, Robot. I saw&nbsp; what happened when the blue light turns red,&nbsp;&nbsp; and I’m not looking forward to&nbsp; it. That’s where we’re headed.&nbsp; You wonder why we have so much mental&nbsp; illness in our society. Every single day,&nbsp;&nbsp; even if you’re doing everything right, you’ll&nbsp; get a notice like, “Disconnection notice:&nbsp;&nbsp; if you don’t send your payment right away, we’ll&nbsp; turn off your power,” instead of, “Hey, you’re&nbsp;&nbsp; running a little late, dude. Don’t forget to&nbsp; send it in.” That’s never how it is. In America,&nbsp;&nbsp; you’re a hammer, and everything looks like a nail.&nbsp; The people who run the country are no different.&nbsp; I’m glad you like my theory. I was pretty&nbsp; proud of it. Let’s talk about your good friend,&nbsp;&nbsp; the walrus. I am the walrus. Mr. Bolton. He got&nbsp; raided a couple of weeks ago, August 22. Now&nbsp;&nbsp; we know what it was all about. The feds grabbed&nbsp; three computers, two iPhones, and a bunch of old&nbsp;&nbsp; documents. It turns out he was apparently sending&nbsp; a lot of classified information to family members.&nbsp;&nbsp; That’s what this investigation is about. He’s in&nbsp; trouble, right? It sounds like he legitimately&nbsp;&nbsp; did some illegal stuff. That’s literally the&nbsp; definition of espionage in the Espionage Act.&nbsp; The definition of espionage was mainstreamed in&nbsp; my case. It set a precedent: providing national&nbsp;&nbsp; defense information to any person not entitled&nbsp; to receive it. The feds raided Bolton’s Bethesda&nbsp;&nbsp; home and Washington, D.C. office as part of an&nbsp; investigation into allegations that he snuck&nbsp;&nbsp; national security files out of the White House&nbsp; during President Trump’s first term by emailing&nbsp;&nbsp; them to family members on a private server.&nbsp; Is John Bolton stupid? I know he’s not very&nbsp;&nbsp; smart when it comes to foreign policy. He’s&nbsp; arrogant—that’s what it is. Edward Snowden&nbsp;&nbsp; used thumb drives. That’s a lot faster. You&nbsp; can find out if data has been taken that way,&nbsp;&nbsp; but if you’re doing it by email, it’s going to&nbsp; go through a server. It’s going to be known,&nbsp;&nbsp; right? It’s impossible that it won’t be&nbsp; known soon. The FBI is saying they took&nbsp;&nbsp; all his electronics, which they normally do.&nbsp; On the back of my computer that I’m talking&nbsp;&nbsp; to you through, I still have an FBI sticker that&nbsp; says “evidence” on it. At first, I couldn’t get&nbsp;&nbsp; it off, and now I keep it as a souvenir. The FBI also confiscated two USB drives,&nbsp;&nbsp; a hard drive, four boxes marked “printed daily&nbsp; activities,” typed documents in folders labeled&nbsp;&nbsp; “Trump one through four,” and a white binder&nbsp; labeled “statements and reflections to allied&nbsp;&nbsp; strikes.” The warrant specifically accuses&nbsp; him of violating two different sections of&nbsp;&nbsp; the Espionage Act. So yeah, this is real.&nbsp; He’s in real trouble. Do you think he sent&nbsp;&nbsp; the stuff to his family to brag or just to get it&nbsp; to himself, thinking he wouldn’t get caught? This&nbsp;&nbsp; is for his memoirs. I think he probably did&nbsp; it to make it easier for him to access later.&nbsp;&nbsp; This is not advice to anyone, but if I were&nbsp; going to do this, I would take screenshots&nbsp;&nbsp; with my phone of the computer screen. That’s what&nbsp; I would do. Then I could toss the phone. Although&nbsp;&nbsp; that’s how they caught the FBI whistleblower from&nbsp; Minneapolis—his name escapes me. But couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp; you use a phone not attached to the internet?&nbsp; Turn it off, use it as a camera. Hell, bring a&nbsp;&nbsp; camera—old school. But this is the arrogance&nbsp; we’d expect from someone like John Bolton.&nbsp; He’s old and not looking good at all. He&nbsp; looks really worn out. I guess you probably&nbsp;&nbsp; have some sense of what that’s like when you&nbsp; go through something like that. Starline’s&nbsp;&nbsp; asking if you ever got a chance to work with&nbsp; John Perry Barlow, co-founder of the EFF,&nbsp;&nbsp; the Electronic Frontier Foundation. No, I’ve spoken to him a couple of times,&nbsp;&nbsp; but I never had a chance to work with him. I’ve&nbsp; been told, for whatever reason, I’m not the&nbsp;&nbsp; most popular person at the EFF. I don’t know why,&nbsp; because I always liked and respected them. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp; how it was for me. I found out the same thing&nbsp; about me. I had every First Amendment organization&nbsp;&nbsp; in the United States on my side, sending amicus&nbsp; briefs against the LA Times, which is something&nbsp;&nbsp; because they’re a newspaper, and normally they’d&nbsp; be on their side. The two big exceptions were the&nbsp;&nbsp; EFF, who said, “You’re not popular here,” and&nbsp; the ACLU of Southern California, because they&nbsp;&nbsp; had a deal with the LA Times. The ACLU national&nbsp; would have been on my side, but the ACLU makes&nbsp;&nbsp; decisions regionally. A member of the board of the&nbsp; ACLU resigned in protest of the ACLU not making&nbsp;&nbsp; a statement in support of me during my trial. I got a letter. They’re [ __ ]. Not as big a&nbsp;&nbsp; bunch of [ __ ] as Amnesty International. Amnesty&nbsp; International refused to say a single word in&nbsp;&nbsp; support of me in my darkest days of 2012. This was Amnesty International London,&nbsp;&nbsp; the headquarters. The head of Amnesty&nbsp; International United States, a guy named Zeke,&nbsp;&nbsp; I think his last name was Johnson, an awesome guy,&nbsp; wrote me a letter when I was in prison apologizing&nbsp;&nbsp; for his bosses in London. Then he bought me a&nbsp; subscription to The New Yorker to dull the pain,&nbsp;&nbsp; I guess. It was only later, when I was getting&nbsp; out of prison, that Ai Weiwei, the Chinese artist,&nbsp;&nbsp; wanted to do my portrait for a show at Alcatraz.&nbsp; He made 178 portraits out of Legos of 178&nbsp;&nbsp; political prisoners around the world. He wanted&nbsp; to clear the names with Amnesty International&nbsp;&nbsp; first. Amnesty said, “We don’t believe Kiriakou is&nbsp; a political prisoner.” He said, “I know Kiriakou.&nbsp;&nbsp; I’m telling you, he is a political prisoner.” They&nbsp; finally agreed, and the week I got out of prison,&nbsp;&nbsp; Amnesty International finally said, “Okay, you&nbsp; were a political prisoner.” But they had to be&nbsp;&nbsp; pulled kicking and screaming to that point of&nbsp; view. They were real jerks about it. Sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp; it is like that when you’re fighting for your life&nbsp; and making calls to these groups. It’s amazing. I&nbsp;&nbsp; ended up with eight amicus briefs, but some of&nbsp; them came through better than others. You’re&nbsp;&nbsp; literally like, “Are you [ __ ] serious? You’re&nbsp; not going to side with me? Really? You know what&nbsp;&nbsp; they did?” They’re like, “Yeah, but…” That’s&nbsp; what you’re for. That’s why you get donations.&nbsp;&nbsp; I used to donate to the ACLU. They had a cartoon&nbsp; auction every year, and I always used to bid on a&nbsp;&nbsp; cartoon. I’m like, “Thanks a lot, [ __ ].&nbsp; No more cartoons from me.” Unbelievable,&nbsp;&nbsp; these people. Somebody told me the other day&nbsp; there’s a very prominent attorney here in&nbsp;&nbsp; Washington. A mutual friend told me, “He doesn’t&nbsp; think you’re a whistleblower.” I said, “Isn’t it&nbsp;&nbsp; wonderful to be at a point in my life where&nbsp; I don’t give a [ __ ] what he thinks about me?”&nbsp; That is nice. When you interview me, I’ll get into&nbsp; all the nuts and bolts. By the way, for people&nbsp;&nbsp; watching, we’re now dropping our interview with&nbsp; John for Rumble Premium. It’s great. We’re going&nbsp;&nbsp; to do probably two more hours and then do mine.&nbsp; When we do mine, when you hear how this all went&nbsp;&nbsp; down, it was proven by science. But there were&nbsp; still some people—like a cartoonist, who shall&nbsp;&nbsp; remain nameless, except he was the cartoonist&nbsp; at the Salt Lake City paper for many years&nbsp;&nbsp; and probably still is. He was telling my friend&nbsp; Scott Stantis, “I don’t know. I think Ted faked&nbsp;&nbsp; that evidence.” It’s like, what do you think? Do&nbsp; you think I have the power of a major espionage&nbsp;&nbsp; agency to break into the LAPD vault, alter their&nbsp; evidence, and use it to exonerate myself? If I had&nbsp;&nbsp; that power, I wouldn’t be here doing podcasts and&nbsp; drawing cartoons. It’s absurd, but people believe&nbsp;&nbsp; what they want to believe. That’s depressing. It is nice to get to the point where you just&nbsp;&nbsp; don’t care. Reed’s a dude, by the way. My&nbsp; apologies. Fetterman is going through a&nbsp;&nbsp; serious mental health crisis, still, and I&nbsp; guess it’s getting worse. I can’t believe&nbsp;&nbsp; that guy’s still in the Senate. John, you’re from&nbsp; Pennsylvania. How do you feel about that? I think&nbsp;&nbsp; it’s [ __ ] atrocious. It’s a travesty, and I’m&nbsp; furious about it. Although, I’ll tell you why he’s&nbsp;&nbsp; still in the Senate: he has maintained the strong&nbsp; support of organized labor. He hasn’t budged on&nbsp;&nbsp; his support for organized labor. He has become&nbsp; what Republicans are now calling Donald Trump’s&nbsp;&nbsp; favorite Democrat. In a state like Pennsylvania,&nbsp; which goes back and forth between the two parties,&nbsp;&nbsp; that’s the position you want to be in. People&nbsp; like you and I have zero respect for the guy,&nbsp;&nbsp; but they don’t care what people like you and I&nbsp; think. The union members are going to vote for&nbsp;&nbsp; him, and Republicans are going to vote for him.&nbsp; That’s why he’s able to do this. With that said,&nbsp;&nbsp; the question you just put up on the screen—why&nbsp; is John Fetterman still in the Senate? That’s the&nbsp;&nbsp; question. If he is mentally and emotionally&nbsp; unable to serve, and apparently physically,&nbsp;&nbsp; because he literally doesn’t show up to&nbsp; votes and doesn’t interact with constituents,&nbsp;&nbsp; his staff can’t keep any staff members because&nbsp; he’s crazy, and they run away. He’s mean. I told&nbsp;&nbsp; you I saw him at that Ohio State football game. He&nbsp; was ten rows in front of me, and I kept shouting,&nbsp;&nbsp; “Fetterman, I want my 50 bucks back.” He could hear his name, but because of&nbsp;&nbsp; the crowd, he couldn’t really hear what I was&nbsp; saying. So he would just turn around and go.&nbsp; Ugh, that guy. He’s such a dick. The funny part is&nbsp; that he’s become even more maniacally pro-Zionist.&nbsp;&nbsp; It’s irrational. Maybe nowadays, to be Zionist,&nbsp; you literally have to be insane. Yes, indeed.&nbsp;&nbsp; Matthew Blair Rains has a good question. Thank&nbsp; you for the 10 pounds, Matthew. Why did the UK&nbsp;&nbsp; and France have such different approaches to their&nbsp; former colonies and empire, both with regards to&nbsp;&nbsp; military and intelligence actions? That’s a great&nbsp; question. I’ll say that the only thing the French&nbsp;&nbsp; really care about is not being attacked by people&nbsp; who are from what used to be colonies. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp; happy to go to the Sahel and try to fight&nbsp; Al-Qaeda, but otherwise, they’re willing to&nbsp;&nbsp; just get up and walk away. The French approach to&nbsp; post-colonialization has been, “If there’s a fire,&nbsp;&nbsp; we’ll come in with the French Foreign Legion and&nbsp; put it out, then go home.” The Brits don’t want to&nbsp;&nbsp; do that. They had the Commonwealth. The illusion&nbsp; is that, technically speaking, the king’s face is&nbsp;&nbsp; still on the money in Canada, Australia, Belize,&nbsp; the Bahamas, and all these ostensibly independent&nbsp;&nbsp; places. The Brits have trouble letting go. They&nbsp; want to think they’re still a colonial power.&nbsp;&nbsp; I think that’s part of the Brexit mentality,&nbsp; ironically, even though the people who voted&nbsp;&nbsp; for it probably weren’t thinking like that at all.&nbsp; It’s cultural. The French think they have majesty&nbsp;&nbsp; because they’re France and that they bestowed&nbsp; some of their majesty on their colonies. The Brits&nbsp;&nbsp; think their majesty comes from having colonies.&nbsp; That’s a good point. That makes sense to me.&nbsp;&nbsp; Big Boss Bob Ross has a good question here: Why&nbsp; did the French help the genocidal Hutu government&nbsp;&nbsp; during the Rwanda genocide? I never understood a&nbsp; single aspect of that genocide. There were Hutus&nbsp;&nbsp; and Tutsis married into each other’s families,&nbsp; living next door to each other for generations,&nbsp;&nbsp; and then, like flipping a switch, they all started&nbsp; killing each other. I just didn’t understand it.&nbsp; Can you make heads or tails of this? Yeah, it was&nbsp; a classic divide-and-conquer strategy. The whole&nbsp;&nbsp; idea that there’s even such a thing as Hutus and&nbsp; Tutsis was invented by the colonial powers. It was&nbsp;&nbsp; a traditional approach to take a favored minority&nbsp; group and put them in charge, kind of like&nbsp;&nbsp; the model the Brits pursued in Iraq. Support&nbsp; the minority; they’ll fight hard to oppress the&nbsp;&nbsp; majority, but the majority will be resentful and&nbsp; seething, and they’ll fight each other. It’s like&nbsp;&nbsp; Stalin when he drew the borders of the Central&nbsp; Asian Republics. Forty percent of Uzbekistan&nbsp;&nbsp; was Tajik, forty percent of Tajikistan was Uzbek,&nbsp; on purpose, because he thought the Fergana Valley&nbsp;&nbsp; Muslims were crazy and would rebel against the&nbsp; Soviet Union. He wasn’t really wrong about that.&nbsp; We don’t have all the time in the world to teach&nbsp; a class on post-colonialism, but that’s the short&nbsp;&nbsp; version. What do we think of Ibrahim Traoré? I&nbsp; know him. He’s a young military officer in Burkina&nbsp;&nbsp; Faso who took power two, three, or four years ago. Yeah, from the French. He overthrew the government&nbsp;&nbsp; and took power. He’s become, in my view,&nbsp; increasingly irrational over the last couple&nbsp;&nbsp; of months. First, he took power, and it’s been at&nbsp; least three years now with no transition back to&nbsp;&nbsp; democracy, which he promised at the outset. Didn’t&nbsp; happen. He’s the military dictator. That’s what&nbsp;&nbsp; they always do. They say, “We’ll have an election&nbsp; as soon as things settle down.” Just recently, he&nbsp;&nbsp; outlawed homosexuality, so if you’re gay,&nbsp; you risk ten years in prison. He also&nbsp;&nbsp; did away with the country’s Electoral Commission&nbsp; in Burkina Faso. I don’t even know why I know&nbsp;&nbsp; this much about Burkina Faso, but they have this&nbsp; permanent Electoral Commission whose job is to&nbsp;&nbsp; plan each election every four years. He scrapped&nbsp; it, fired everybody, got rid of everybody. He&nbsp;&nbsp; took a Trump approach: fire everyone. There aren’t&nbsp; going to be any elections. Also recently, I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp; remember where I saw this, but he started to expel&nbsp; foreigners—a program to expel all foreigners from&nbsp;&nbsp; Burkina Faso. Burkina Faso was never a garden&nbsp; spot, but man, it’s getting worse and worse.&nbsp; Military dictatorship, always fun. So, do we want&nbsp; to talk about Salt Typhoon, or should we call it&nbsp;&nbsp; a night? Let’s say something quickly about Salt&nbsp; Typhoon. The Chinese are really good at computer&nbsp;&nbsp; attacks. They’ve got this unit within the Chinese&nbsp; intelligence service that only does cyber warfare.&nbsp; They hit the jackpot with this thing, Salt&nbsp; Typhoon, where they hacked the information&nbsp;&nbsp; of literally every American. A couple of outlets&nbsp; today and yesterday were saying the real targets&nbsp;&nbsp; were Donald Trump and JD Vance, but then they&nbsp; thought, “Let’s cast a wide net and see how&nbsp;&nbsp; much success we can have.” They had complete&nbsp; success, collecting data from every single&nbsp;&nbsp; American. They’re good, and our defenses are not&nbsp; so good. It seems like every few months or weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp; we wake up to some data breach: Target lost all&nbsp; their credit card data, or everyone’s social&nbsp;&nbsp; security numbers are on the dark web. China&nbsp; has everything. You use a password somewhere,&nbsp;&nbsp; and you’re warned, “Change your password;&nbsp; it exists on the dark web.” Most people,&nbsp;&nbsp; I don’t think I’m alone, just throw up their hands&nbsp; and say, “[ __ ] it. There’s no way to safeguard my&nbsp;&nbsp; data anyway. I can’t be bothered. If people&nbsp; want to steal my identity, they can have it.”&nbsp; It seems like there’s no resources, nothing you&nbsp; can even do about it. John, I can’t remember a&nbsp;&nbsp; single politician giving a speech saying,&nbsp; “This is a major concern, and we probably&nbsp;&nbsp; ought to have some committees and talk about this&nbsp; stuff and do something about it.” It just happens&nbsp;&nbsp; in the paper, you read it, and you’re like, “Oh,&nbsp; okay,” and there’s no leadership whatsoever.&nbsp; You’re absolutely right. Marble, thanks again.&nbsp; Marble’s got one other question: John and Ted, why&nbsp;&nbsp; did Russia recognize the Taliban government? I’ve&nbsp; got a theory. They did it for a couple of reasons:&nbsp;&nbsp; one, to put Afghanistan finally behind them. The&nbsp; real reason is because the United States hasn’t,&nbsp;&nbsp; and this is another opportunity for Russia or&nbsp; China to move in where the U.S. has been too&nbsp;&nbsp; reluctant, maybe negotiate a sweetheart deal&nbsp; on rare earth metals or establish new trade&nbsp;&nbsp; relations. It couldn’t hurt with BRICS. If you&nbsp; want trade relations between Russia and India,&nbsp;&nbsp; it’s the Silk Road. The Taliban are very good at&nbsp; providing security on the ground. Trucks can make&nbsp;&nbsp; it across into Uzbekistan or Turkmenistan and&nbsp; then on to Russia, and vice versa. Pipelines.&nbsp;&nbsp; I wrote an entire book about the Trans-Afghanistan&nbsp; Pipeline Project that Bill Clinton and Unocal&nbsp;&nbsp; tried to start in the mid-90s. That could be&nbsp; revived now. The problem was you could never&nbsp;&nbsp; run a pipeline across such a restive place&nbsp; where tribesmen would constantly threaten to&nbsp;&nbsp; blow it up. I think it’s a good thing. Honestly,&nbsp; I don’t think diplomatic recognition should be a&nbsp;&nbsp; reward or something. Every country should have&nbsp; diplomatic relations with every other country,&nbsp;&nbsp; period. Let the diplomats do what they’re paid to&nbsp; do—carry out diplomacy. We should have relations&nbsp;&nbsp; with everybody that wants relations with us.&nbsp; The truth is, when we need to talk to people,&nbsp;&nbsp; we end up talking to them anyway. When I needed a visa to go to Iran,&nbsp;&nbsp; we don’t have diplomatic relations, but Iran had&nbsp; an interest section in the Pakistani embassy in&nbsp;&nbsp; Washington. That’s what my fixer went through&nbsp; to get it. The lack of diplomatic relations&nbsp;&nbsp; just makes it harder for them to do their jobs.&nbsp; I’m proud to say—I’ve not said this publicly,&nbsp;&nbsp; but I’m going to say it now—I’m one of six people&nbsp; invited to meet with Iranian President Pezeshkian&nbsp;&nbsp; in New York when he arrives for the UN General&nbsp; Assembly. I said yes without even asking what he&nbsp;&nbsp; wanted to talk about. That’s great. I’m excited&nbsp; about it. See if they’ll do an interview. The&nbsp;&nbsp; worst that could happen is he says no. Why not? I heard back from them today. They don’t yet&nbsp;&nbsp; have a date or time, but there are six of us&nbsp; he wants to talk to. Medea Benjamin is one,&nbsp;&nbsp; Max Blumenthal is one, Andy Shalal is one, me,&nbsp; and I don’t know who the other two are. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp; awesome. Iala wants to know what Afghanistan has&nbsp; in terms of natural resources, raw materials.&nbsp; I can answer this. In the north, there’s&nbsp; oil—not a lot, but a decent amount worth&nbsp;&nbsp; having. In the east, there are rare earth&nbsp; minerals and gemstones, particularly rubies,&nbsp;&nbsp; as you and I both know. That’s basically it.&nbsp; During the first Taliban period from 1996 to 2001,&nbsp;&nbsp; they were so poor that their number one official&nbsp; export was pomegranates. You’re right about all&nbsp;&nbsp; the other stuff. They have twenty-three trillion&nbsp; dollars’ worth of rare earth metals. We learned&nbsp;&nbsp; that just as U.S. forces were pulling out and&nbsp; Chinese miners were moving in. Their rubies&nbsp;&nbsp; are some of the best in the world. It’s pretty&nbsp; impressive. At the very least, we should have&nbsp;&nbsp; an embassy open there, even if it’s just two&nbsp; or three people, and we should be carrying out&nbsp;&nbsp; diplomacy constantly. Not to mention, we still&nbsp; have some Americans trapped there who have no&nbsp;&nbsp; consular representation. We abandoned them. I don’t understand why President Trump hasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp; restored those relationships. I agree with&nbsp; you. He should do it. All right, guys,&nbsp;&nbsp; thank you so much for joining us. It’s always&nbsp; appreciated. We’ll be back tomorrow at 5 o’clock&nbsp;&nbsp; Eastern Time. We’re here Monday through Friday.&nbsp; You’re watching DeProgram with Ted Rall and&nbsp;&nbsp; John Kiriakou. Please like, follow, and share the&nbsp; show. We’ll see you tomorrow at 5. Bye, everybody.


